---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { TESTS } from '../data/tests';
import { article, faqPage } from '../lib/schema';
import { SITE_BUILD_DATE } from '../lib/dates';

const PATH = '/methodology/';
const totalItems = TESTS.reduce((n, t) => n + t.questions.length, 0);
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How are Upfront\u2019s tests scored?', answer: 'Each item contributes a signed value from -2 to +2, multiplied by its keying direction. Those values are summed per scale and expressed as a position between the two poles, where 50% is an exact tie. Preference strength is the distance from that midpoint, doubled, so it runs from 0 to 100.' },
  { question: 'What is reverse keying and why does it matter?', answer: 'Half the items on every scale are worded so that agreeing moves the score in the opposite direction. Without this, a respondent who agrees with everything — a common and well-documented response style — would be pushed to an extreme that reflects their response habit rather than their personality.' },
  { question: 'Are these tests validated?', answer: 'They are not formally normed or peer-reviewed, and we do not claim otherwise. They are built on published frameworks using standard psychometric construction practice: balanced keying, adequate items per scale, and continuous rather than binary reporting. For decisions with real consequences, use a properly normed instrument administered by a qualified practitioner.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How Upfront Builds and Scores Its Tests"
  description="The item design, keying balance, and scoring maths behind every Upfront assessment — including what these tests are not validated to do."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Methodology', path: PATH }]}
  schema={[
    article({ headline: 'How Upfront builds and scores its tests', description: 'Item design, keying and scoring methodology.', path: PATH, published: SITE_BUILD_DATE, modified: SITE_BUILD_DATE, section: 'About' }),
    faqPage(faqs, PATH),
  ]}
  layout="narrow"
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How these tests are built and scored</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      {totalItems} items across {TESTS.length} assessments, with the construction decisions
      stated openly — including the ones that limit what the results can be used for.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2>Item design</h2>
    <p>
      Every scale is measured by at least six statements, and the personality type test uses
      twelve per dimension. More items per scale means a more stable score: a single ambiguous
      question cannot swing the result.
    </p>
    <p>
      Items are written as concrete behavioural statements rather than abstract self-descriptions.
      "I often think of what I wanted to say only after the conversation has ended" is easier to
      answer accurately than "I am an introvert", because it asks about something that either
      happens to you or does not.
    </p>

    <h2>Balanced keying</h2>
    <p>
      Half the items on every scale are reverse-keyed — worded so that agreement moves the score
      toward the opposite pole. This is not stylistic. It controls for
      <strong>acquiescence bias</strong>, the well-documented tendency for some respondents to
      agree with statements regardless of content.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical effect: if you answered "strongly agree" to all 48 questions on the
      personality type test, you would score exactly 50% on every dimension — an honest "no
      preference detected" rather than a flattering extreme. Many casual online quizzes score
      that same response pattern as a maximally strong personality.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        This property is covered by an automated test that runs on every change to the site, so
        it cannot quietly regress if items are edited later.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>The scoring maths</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Each response maps to a value from <code>-2</code> (strongly disagree) to <code>+2</code> (strongly agree).</li>
      <li>That value is multiplied by the item's keying direction, <code>+1</code> or <code>-1</code>.</li>
      <li>Values are summed across the items belonging to one scale.</li>
      <li>The sum is converted to a position between the two poles, where <strong>50% is an exact tie</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Preference strength</strong> is the distance from 50, doubled — so 0 means a perfect tie and 100 means every item was answered at the extreme in the same direction.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Unanswered items are skipped rather than counted as neutral, so a partially completed scale
      is scored on what was actually answered. In practice the tests require every item before
      showing a result.
    </p>

    <h2>Reporting strength, not just a label</h2>
    <p>
      Every result leads with preference strength. A four-letter type where one dimension scored
      3% is a fundamentally different result from one where every dimension scored 80%, and
      reporting only the letters hides that completely.
      <a href="/guides/what-do-personality-test-percentages-mean/">Why this is the most misread part of any result.</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy by construction</h2>
    <p>
      Scoring runs in your browser. The site is static files with no backend, so your answers are
      never transmitted — not because of a policy, but because there is no endpoint to transmit
      them to. Nothing is stored, and no email address is requested at any point.
    </p>

    <h2>What these tests are not</h2>
    <p>
      They are not normed against a reference population, not peer-reviewed, and not validated
      for selection, diagnosis, or clinical use. They are educational instruments for
      self-reflection.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Do not use them for hiring.</strong> Type-based instruments do not reliably predict job performance. Structured interviews and work samples do it far better.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not use them diagnostically.</strong> They measure preference, not pathology.</li>
      <li><strong>Treat the result as a hypothesis</strong> about how you tend to operate, and check it against how you actually behave.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The longer version of this argument, including where the research disagrees with the
      framework, is in <a href="/guides/are-personality-tests-accurate/">are personality tests actually accurate</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    {faqs.map((faq) => (
      <details><summary>{faq.question}</summary><p>{faq.answer}</p></details>
    ))}
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
